6.8 Unit roots and the Dickey-Fuller test
Let us go back to our \(AR(1)\) model without constant, \(Y_{t}=\rho Y_{t-1}+u_{t}\). We have seen that with \(\rho =1\) the series is non-stationary; \(\rho &lt;1\) poses no such problem, while we do not consider \(\rho &gt;1\), since the series would follow an unrealistic explosive path (Figure 6.7), inconsistent moreover with our working assumptions and with the meaning attributed to \(\rho\). We set aside for now the case \(-1&lt;\rho &lt;0\) and, as with values greater than one, we do not consider \(\rho &lt;-1\).

RPythonSAS



u &lt;- rnorm(500)
Y &lt;- 0
for (i in 2:500) Y[i] &lt;- 1.03*Y[i-1]+u[i]
plot(Y, type="l", main="Explosive series with coefficient 1.03")




Figure 6.7 – Explosive series







u = rng.standard_normal(500)
Y = np.zeros(500)
for i in range(1, 500):
    Y[i] = 1.03 * Y[i - 1] + u[i]
plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))
plt.plot(Y, color="black")
plt.title("Explosive series with coefficient 1.03")
plt.show()




Figure 6.7 – Explosive series






data esplosiva;
  call streaminit(321);
  Y = 0;
  do t = 1 to 500;
    if t &gt; 1 then Y = 1.03*Y + rand("normal");
    output;
  end;
run;
proc sgplot data=esplosiva;
  title "Explosive series with coefficient 1.03";
  series x=t y=Y;
run;



The question now is how to test whether \(\rho =1\), that is, whether there is a unit root. The test used is the Dickey-Fuller test, for three possible versions of the suitably reparameterised model:


without drift: \(\Delta Y_{t}=\delta Y_{t-1}+u_{t}\)


with drift: \(\Delta Y_{t}=\beta_{1}+\delta Y_{t-1}+u_{t}\)


with drift around a trend: \(\Delta Y_{t}=\beta_{1}+\beta_{2}t+\delta Y_{t-1}+u_{t}\)


where in all cases \(\delta =\rho -1\).
In particular, the Dickey-Fuller test tests the null hypothesis \(\delta =0\) (there is a unit root, so the series is not stationary). Under the more general assumption that the residuals \(u_{t}\) may be correlated, the augmented version of the three models is preferred, which includes among the explanatory variables the lagged differences of the dependent variable up to some lag. The test on \(\delta\), known as the Dickey-Fuller (\(DF\)) or augmented Dickey-Fuller (\(ADF\)) test, does not follow Student’s t even in large samples, and a specific \(\tau\) distribution must be used to assess its significance.

6.11 Simulating AR(1) processes
So far we have presented some autoregressive forms, almost always specifying first-order models with a positive autoregressive coefficient (preferably below one), and justifying this choice by the cases most commonly found in macroeconomic reality. Nevertheless, it is useful to see how an AR process behaves with very different values of \(\rho\). We simulate 100 observations from the \(AR(1)\) model without constant \(Y_{t}=\rho Y_{t-1}+u_{t}\), with values of \(\rho\) also outside the range usually considered (Figure 6.9).

RPythonSAS



u &lt;- rnorm(100); par(mfrow=c(3,3))
coe &lt;- c(0.0,0.3,0.7,1.0,1.1,-0.2,-0.8,-1.0,-1.1)
