SARIMA (seasonal ARIMA) models have correlograms entirely similar to those of non-seasonal ARIMA models; in particular, the autocorrelation functions show cyclical variations at the seasonal lags. In base R (without external packages), arima.sim() cannot simulate seasonal data directly; this is not a real limitation, since a SARIMA is nothing but a suitable combination of two ARIMA models, one applied to the non-seasonal movements of the series and the other to the seasonal fluctuations; we will come back to this shortly with an example.

8.2 The Cobb-Douglas function and returns to scale
In its basic formulation the Cobb-Douglas (CD) is
\[
Y=aL^{\alpha}K^{\beta}\varepsilon
\tag{8.3}
\]
It is therefore non-linear in the parameters and must be linearised by taking logarithms
\[
\log Y=\log a+\alpha \log L+\beta \log K+\mu
\tag{8.4}
\]
At this point OLS estimators can be used, provided, as usual, that the statistical conditions for a good regression estimate hold.
The CD is a homogeneous function of degree \(\alpha +\beta\). In particular, if \(\alpha +\beta =1\) we say there are constant returns to scale; that is, if inputs increase by a factor \(k\), output increases proportionally by the same factor. If \(\alpha +\beta &lt;1\), there are decreasing returns to scale: if inputs increase by a scale factor \(k\), output grows less than proportionally. Conversely, if \(\alpha +\beta &gt;1\), output grows more than proportionally to inputs. Clearly \(\alpha +\beta =1\) is a desirable equilibrium condition, but there is generally no economic or statistical reason to assume that this constraint holds a priori.
We load the data in the file funzprod.csv, on output, labour and capital in the agricultural sector of Taiwan over 1958-1972, expressed in millions of local dollars at constant prices and in millions of working days. We estimate the Cobb-Douglas function linearised in logarithms

